course: dictionary
lesson: 5991
title: Meanie: playful, but not always fair
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A meanie is a person who is unkind, selfish or not generous. Children often use it in a playful way, so it is usually not a serious insult. The words around it give you the clues: «Don't be a meanie, share your sweets!» tells you the speaker wants someone to share.
explain: Context also shows how strong a word feels. A joke between friends sounds different from a cruel remark. Read this: «Some teasing is never fair: calling a crying friend a crybaby, a poor friend a meanie...» The writer says this teasing is unfair, so meanie can still hurt, even when it sounds light.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try «Don't be an unkind person, share your sweets!» If it still makes sense, your meaning is right.
fact: Meanie is an informal word, mostly used by children or as a joke.
fact: «Meanie» means a person who is unkind, selfish or not generous.
fact: Meanie can also be spelled meany, and both spellings mean the same.
fact: Phrases you may hear: «don't be a meanie», «big meanie», «you meanie!»
fact: In Chinese, «xiǎoqìguǐ» means a meanie, someone stingy who won't share.
exercise: «Don't be a meanie, share your sweets!» What does meanie mean here? | A kind friend who gives everything away | *Someone who is not sharing or kind | A sweet shop owner | Someone who is very tall
exercise: Which word is another spelling of meanie? | meat | *meany | meaning | meanness
exercise: «Some teasing is never fair.» Calling a crying friend a crybaby is what kind of teasing? | *Unfair, and it can hurt | A word that means happy | Never used by children | Fair and kind
test: Is meanie a formal or informal word? | Formal, used in school reports | Very old, from a history book | Only used by teachers | *Informal, often used by children or as a joke
test: In the sentence «you meanie!», what feeling does the speaker probably show? | Deep sadness only | *A playful complaint | Great fear | Calm respect
exam: Read: «Don't be a big meanie, Ravi, it's only a game.» What does meanie suggest? | A dangerous threat | A compliment about kindness | A word meaning brave | *A playful telling-off, not a serious insult
